ss \\(z\\) in terms of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):**\n   Let \\(z = x + yi\\), where \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are real numbers.\n\n2. **Substitute \\(z = x + yi\\) into the equation:**\n   \\[\n   2(x + yi) + i = i(x + yi) + 3\n   \\]\n\n3. **Distribute the constants:**\n   \\[\n   2x + 2yi + i = ix + i^2y + 3\n   \\]\n   Recall that \\(i^2 = -1\\), so the equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   2x + 2yi + i = ix - y + 3\n   \\]\n\n4. **Combine like terms:**\n   Group the real parts and the imaginary parts on both sides of the equation:\n   \\[\n   (2x + i) + 2yi = (3 - y) + ix\n   \\]\n\n5. **Separate the equation into real and imaginary parts:**\n   The real part on the left is \\(2x\\) and the imaginary part is \\(2y + 1\\).\n   The real part on the right is \\(3 - y\\) and the imaginary part is \\(x\\).\n   Therefore, we have two equations:\n   \\[\n   2x = 3 - y \\quad \\text{(1)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2y + 1 = x \\quad \\text{(2)}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Solve the system of equations:**\n   From equation (2), solve for \\(x\\):\n   \\[\n   x = 2y + 1\n   \\]\n   Substitute \\(x = 2y + 1\\) into equation (1):\n   \\[\n   2(2y + 1) = 3 - y\n   \\]\n   Simplify and solve for \\(y\\):\n   \\[\n   4y + 2 = 3 - y\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   4y + y = 3 - 2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   5y = 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{1}{5}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Find \\(x\\) using \\(y = \\frac{1}{5}\\):**\n   Substitute \\(y = \\frac{1}{5}\\) back into \\(x = 2y + 1\\):\n   \\[\n   x = 2\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right) + 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{2}{5} + 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{2}{5} + \\frac{5}{5}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{7}{5}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Write the solution in the form \\(x + yi\\):**\n   \\[\n   z = \\frac{7}{5} + \\frac{1}{5}i\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the solution is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{5} + \\frac{1}{5}i}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{7}{5} + \\frac{1}{5}i}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
